: -2.0, "money": -1.62}, "rating_margin": -12.5, "sigma_race": 6.5, "sigma_total": 7.15, "baseline_margin": -8.83, "analyst_adjustment": -1.67, "key_factors": ["Van Duyne is an incumbent in a district that leans Republican by eight points", "Dominant fundraising edge: $2.5M raised and $2.9M on hand versus $210K for Burge", "All ten expert raters place the race in Republican-favorable categories, from Likely to Solid or Safe R", "No public general-election polling, leaving the outlook reliant on fundamentals"], "rationale": "The incumbent combines a Republican-leaning seat with a roughly twelve-to-one fundraising advantage and unanimous favorable ratings, while markets give Burge only about a nine percent chance. With no polling to contradict those signals, the forecast sits between the fundamentals and the most pessimistic rating consensus for Democrats.", "watch": "Any independent polling putting Burge within single digits, or a pronounced late shift in the national environment toward Democrats, would be the clearest sign the race is tightening.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Beth Van Duyne (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Texas's 24th District, in the suburban corridor between Dallas and Fort Worth, leans Republican by about eight points, and Representative Beth Van Duyne is seeking another term as the incumbent. She holds a decisive financial edge, having raised roughly $2.5 million and reporting $2.9 million on hand, while Democratic nominee Kevin Burge has raised about $210,000 with limited cash remaining. Outside groups have spent modestly to support Van Duyne and nothing on the other side.\n\nNo public general-election polls are available, so the outlook rests on district fundamentals, campaign finance, and expert assessments. Rating outlets uniformly place the race in Republican-favorable categories ranging from Likely to Solid or Safe Republican, and pred